postgres.js 3.4.9, two defects after a lost connection.

1. connection.js, execute(): a query run on a connection whose socket is gone.

When the connection closes, closed() sets the socket to null and calls onclose,
which rejects the Promise.race in sql.begin with CONNECTION_CLOSED, so the
transaction rejects to its caller. A ROLLBACK that scope() issues after that,
or any other query run on the closed connection, is still accepted by
execute(): write() schedules nextWrite() with setImmediate, and nextWrite()
throws "Cannot read properties of null (reading 'write')" outside any promise
chain, which ends the process.

This patch rejects such a query with CONNECTION_CLOSED instead, so nothing
is written and the process keeps running. execute() returns true, as for a
query that was accepted: its callers (the handler in index.js) take a falsy
result to mean the connection is busy and move it to the full queue, which
would take a closed connection out of reconnecting.

2. connection.js, closed(): a server error left over from a lost connection.

When a backend is terminated (57P01) while a query runs, ErrorResponse() keeps
the error in errorResponse, because a query is running; only the
ReadyForQuery() that ends that query hands it over, and none follows, because
the server ends the connection instead. closed() rejects the running query
with CONNECTION_CLOSED (or returns early, see below), but leaves errorResponse
set. The next query that takes the connection (index.js connect(), also called
from onclose for a waiting one) becomes its initial query. The ReadyForQuery
that ends the startup of the new socket finds the old error and passes it to
errored(), which rejects that initial query with the old PostgresError. The
first query after the loss then fails with an error of the previous connection,
although only the request that was running when the connection was lost should
fail.

This patch clears errorResponse in closed(), next to the socket.

closed() leaves query set, so the ReadyForQuery that ends the startup of the
next socket still settles the query that was running when the connection was
lost. Without the stored error it resolves that query with the result it holds,
which does nothing once closed() or the socket's error handler has rejected it
with its own connection error.

closed() returns early, before it rejects anything, when initial is set
(reconnect(), src/connection.js:450). That includes a 57P01 during the fetch_types query
that fetchArrayTypes() runs at startup, which is on by default. That query is
then the running one, and it is never rejected with CONNECTION_CLOSED:
 - Before the patch, the ReadyForQuery of the new socket rejected it with the
   stored error and errored() rejected initial too, so the first query on the
   connection failed with the old PostgresError. fetchArrayTypes() is not
   awaited (ReadyForQuery() returns its promise), so the rejection of its
   query was also an unhandled rejection.
 - After the patch, that query resolves with the Result it holds: the rows
   received before the connection was lost, empty if there were none.
   fetchArrayTypes() passes them to addArrayType(), which registers the array
   parsers and serializers of those rows. connected() has set needsTypes again
   for the new socket, so the same ReadyForQuery goes on to start
   fetchArrayTypes() once more, which fetches them all, and initial runs
   after that. No array type stays unregistered, and the first query on the
   connection succeeds.

Upstream: no issue is linked yet; the defect is to be reported to
https://github.com/porsager/postgres. Drop this patch when a release carries
the fix.

diff --git a/cjs/src/connection.js b/cjs/src/connection.js
index 07f6716702ac888215c86ad6e0071aaf55ae519c..9af8512c45680f7a503cd45edf3048c8b31cb79c 100644
--- a/cjs/src/connection.js
+++ b/cjs/src/connection.js
@@ -157,6 +157,11 @@ function Connection(options, queues = {}, { onopen = noop, onend = noop, onclose
     if (terminated)
       return queryError(q, Errors.connection('CONNECTION_DESTROYED', options))
 
+    if (socket === null) {
+      queryError(q, Errors.connection('CONNECTION_CLOSED', options, socket))
+      return true
+    }
+
     if (stream)
       return queryError(q, Errors.generic('COPY_IN_PROGRESS', 'You cannot execute queries during copy'))
 
@@ -446,6 +451,7 @@ function Connection(options, queues = {}, { onopen = noop, onend = noop, onclose
 
     socket.removeAllListeners()
     socket = null
+    errorResponse = null
 
     if (initial)
       return reconnect()
